Gamification rules
==================

This file is the authoritative statement of the scoring rules. Both clients
mirror parts of it for display; if the two ever disagree, the server is right
and the client is the bug.


XP
--

users.totalXp is the only balance. It is a float column and is always changed
with an increment inside the transaction that writes the underlying record.

Sources of XP:

	Gym check-in           75 XP, once per calendar day
	Logged exercise        points computed at log time on the session
	Activity award         unitsLogged * pointsPerUnit, snapshotted
	Quest claim            the quest's xp value
	Challenge approval     pointsAwarded set by staff
	Challenge podium       podiumFirst / Second / Third on the challenge
	Badge award            the badge's awardXpValue

XP is never removed. Deleting a badge cascades the award row away but does not
claw back the XP it granted.


Levels
------

	level = floor(totalXp / 2000) + 1

Defined in src/controllers/meController.ts and mirrored in
src/utils/serializers.ts. Both must change together. /user/me also returns
xpIntoLevel, xpMax (2000) and xpToNext so the client never recomputes the
curve itself.


Avatar tiers
------------

Avatar art changes at tier boundaries, not at every level. Five tiers:

	Tier 1  Squire   levels 1-2
	Tier 2  Knight   levels 3-7
	Tier 3  Hero     levels 8-13
	Tier 4  Legend   levels 14-19
	Tier 5  Lancer   levels 20-25

serializeUser returns both level and avatarTier so any leaderboard renders
correct art without a second call. Which art file is used is the client's
concern: the tier picks the frame within the faculty's set, and the faculty
key picks the set.


Streaks
-------

The streak is derived, not stored. GET /user/me pulls the distinct calendar
days on which the user has an ExerciseSession and walks backwards from today.
If today has no session yet the walk starts at yesterday, so a streak stays
alive until midnight rather than resetting the moment the day rolls over. The
walk is bounded at 730 days.

The consequence is that a streak cannot be desynchronised from reality, and
that deleting sessions rewrites history. user_stats.currentStreak and
longestStreak are legacy and are not the values the app displays.


Daily quests
------------

Three quests are active per day. src/services/dailyQuests.ts resolves them and
is the single source of truth; the badge engine and the endpoint both call it,
deliberately, so the two can never diverge.

Resolution order for a date key "YYYY-MM-DD":

1. If a daily_quest_overrides row exists for that date and at least one of its
   quest ids still resolves to a live quest, those quests are the day's set.
2. Otherwise a deterministic pick of up to three from the bank. The date key
   is hashed into a seed and a linear congruential sequence draws without
   replacement. Same date plus same bank always yields the same three, so no
   per-day state has to be written and every client agrees.

An override whose quests have all been deleted falls back to the deterministic
pick rather than serving an empty day.

Staff edit tomorrow, not today. The admin console shows today's set locked and
the next day editable, which is what the override table is designed for.


Badges
------

Every badge is a row in badges plus a rule row in exactly one family table.
The rule row is what makes it awardable; a badge with no rule row can only be
granted by hand.

Families and where they are evaluated:

	activity   services/badges/activityBadges.ts
	           evaluated in activityController on award-points
	exercise   services/badges/exerciseBadges.ts
	           evaluated in exerciseController on log
	specialty  services/badges/specialtyBadges.ts
	           evaluated in activityController on award-points
	quest      services/badges/questBadges.ts
	           NOT evaluated anywhere yet
	challenge  services/badges/challengeBadges.ts
	           created with the challenge; podium settlement NOT called yet

Awarding always goes through awardBadgeIfNotOwned, which relies on the unique
(userId, badgeID) index to make a repeated evaluation a no-op. Evaluation is
therefore safe to call more than once and safe to call inside the caller's
transaction, which is what every call site does.

Tier and threshold columns let one badge family express bronze/silver/gold
ladders without new tables. Metric names the counter, threshold the value at
which the tier is earned.

The two unwired families are the main outstanding work in this area. See
docs/maintenance.md.